// What the army staff leaves the builders (Scripts/Simulation/ConstructionQueue.cs, Attrition.RebuildTheLost).
{
  // When the staff raises a division for a government - the computer's, or a player's who left the
  // army to the staff - it first leaves this many weeks of the construction bill in the treasury: what
  // the sites the country works at once are paid a day, times seven, times this, never more than the
  // sites still owe. A division costs 0.60; Albania's staff spent 1.20 of a 1.53 treasury on two of them
  // in the first five weeks of 2024 and its factory site stood still for the rest of the year. Four
  // weeks is one monthly budget cycle: long enough for a falling treasury to show in the budget before
  // the builders stop, short enough that a country with a long queue still keeps an army. At war,
  // when a country may borrow for its army, no reserve is kept. 0 keeps none in peace either (the staff
  // still checks the division's real price).
  "ArmyStaffConstructionReserveWeeks": 4.0,

  // How many sites a country works at once (ConstructionCost.WorkingSites): this base, plus one per
  // so many civilian factories standing in its regions, never more than the maximum. The factories
  // are its contractors, as civilian factories are construction capacity in Hearts of Iron. The old
  // rule (economy size x 0.7, rounded) gave one site to every economy under 17 units of weekly
  // output - Italy, France, Germany, Poland - and left 70 % of the world's sites standing in line.
  // SimBench --construction, 15 November 2026: Italy 1 -> 2, France and Germany 1 -> 3, China 8 -> 21.
  "ConstructionSitesBase": 2.0,
  "ConstructionSitesPerCivilianIndustry": 0.25,
  "ConstructionSitesMax": 30,

  // Every catalogue price and build time times these (common/buildings stays as written, so the
  // ratios between buildings hold and a mod moves one number). A civilian factory: 240 -> 180 days.
  "ConstructionCostScale": 0.6,
  "ConstructionTimeScale": 0.75,

  // The most construction may draw from the treasury in a week, as a share of the week's budget
  // income; sites past it wait for the next day's money and say so. 0 = no ceiling (the treasury
  // alone decides). Three-year A/B over seeds 1, 7, 42 (docs/CONSTRUCTION_ECONOMY_REWORK_RU.md):
  // with it, half as many countries sit with every site frozen for lack of money.
  "ConstructionBudgetShare": 0.35,
  // ...plus one part in this many of the treasury a week, so savings are built with (Albania's 0.75
  // at 35 % of a 0.014 income alone built an arms plant at a seventh of its speed) and the ceiling,
  // a fraction of what is left each week, can never empty the treasury. 0: income share alone.
  "ConstructionTreasuryDrawWeeks": 26
}
